Your request of 10:50 is answered in team/historian/reviews/rc1-voice.md: where manuscript/ is not how the editor would say it, by the newsroom writer's rules, file and line, a plainer fix for each.

Already right: no em-dashes, British spelling, no private person, no model named in prose. The five patterns: third person everywhere (a global change); superlatives that become self-praise in his mouth (most rows); passages that speak for him, which in his voice become questions to him; sources described rather than named and dated; chapters that open on their argument before the events.

Two decisions only he can make: his guide of 9 October has the model in its title (chapter 11b), so naming the source and never naming the model clash; and Appendix A's git commands filter by the agents' commit author, which is the model's name.

Two facts found on the way: chapter 13:101 says the $17.4M figure was said on stage (chapter 10 and the transcript: on the slide, never spoken); chapter 13:107's "caught before it reached a slide (C030)" needs the researcher.

